§ `Schedule 1: construction of certain expressions relating to offences`

-- (a) indictable offence: triable on indictment, "whether it is exclusively so triable or triable either way".
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 counts the offence as an indictable offence` `triable on indictment only`
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 counts the offence as an indictable offence` `triable either on indictment or summarily`
#ASSERT NOT `Schedule 1 counts the offence as an indictable offence` `triable only summarily`
-- (b) summary offence: triable only summarily.
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 counts the offence as a summary offence` `triable only summarily`
#ASSERT NOT `Schedule 1 counts the offence as a summary offence` `triable on indictment only`
#ASSERT NOT `Schedule 1 counts the offence as a summary offence` `triable either on indictment or summarily`
-- (c) offence triable either way: other than an offence triable on indictment only by virtue of Part V of the Criminal Justice Act 1988.
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 counts the offence as an offence triable either way` `triable either on indictment or summarily` FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Schedule 1 counts the offence as an offence triable either way` `triable either on indictment or summarily` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Schedule 1 counts the offence as an offence triable either way` `triable on indictment only` FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Schedule 1 counts the offence as an offence triable either way` `triable only summarily` FALSE
-- "and the terms indictable, summary and triable either way, in their application to offences, are to be construed accordingly".
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 construes the term as applied to an offence triable in England and Wales` `indictable` `triable on indictment only` FALSE
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 construes the term as applied to an offence triable in England and Wales` `indictable` `triable either on indictment or summarily` FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Schedule 1 construes the term as applied to an offence triable in England and Wales` `indictable` `triable only summarily` FALSE
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 construes the term as applied to an offence triable in England and Wales` `summary` `triable only summarily` FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Schedule 1 construes the term as applied to an offence triable in England and Wales` `summary` `triable on indictment only` FALSE
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 construes the term as applied to an offence triable in England and Wales` `triable either way` `triable either on indictment or summarily` FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Schedule 1 construes the term as applied to an offence triable in England and Wales` `triable either way` `triable either on indictment or summarily` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Schedule 1 construes the term as applied to an offence triable in England and Wales` `triable either way` `triable only summarily` FALSE
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 construes the term as applied to an offence triable in England and Wales` `indictable offence` `triable either on indictment or summarily` FALSE
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 construes the term as applied to an offence triable in England and Wales` `summary offence` `triable only summarily` FALSE
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 construes the term as applied to an offence triable in England and Wales` `offence triable either way` `triable either on indictment or summarily` FALSE
-- The way an offence is triable is read without regard to section 22 of the Magistrates' Courts Act 1980 in the particular case.
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 reads the way an offence is triable without regard to section 22 of the Magistrates' Courts Act 1980` `triable either on indictment or summarily` `triable only summarily` EQUALS `triable either on indictment or summarily`
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 reads the way an offence is triable without regard to section 22 of the Magistrates' Courts Act 1980` `triable on indictment only` `triable only summarily` EQUALS `triable on indictment only`
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 reads the way an offence is triable without regard to section 22 of the Magistrates' Courts Act 1980` `triable only summarily` `triable on indictment only` EQUALS `triable only summarily`
-- "general limit in a magistrates' court" means the limit laid down by section 224(1) of the Sentencing Code; the limit is an input (12 is an illustration).
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the general limit in a magistrates' court` TRUE (JUST 12) EQUALS 12
#ASSERT REFUSED `Schedule 1 — the general limit in a magistrates' court` FALSE (JUST 12) BECAUSE "Schedule 1 gives no general limit in a magistrates' court for an offence that is not triable either way under the law of England and Wales"
#ASSERT REFUSED `Schedule 1 — the general limit in a magistrates' court` TRUE NOTHING BECAUSE "Schedule 1 points at section 224(1) of the Sentencing Code for the general limit, and the sources do not give it, so the caller must say what it is"

